@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Simple CTE";
}

<div class="container-fluid px-4 py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            Simple CTE
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how to create and use a simple Common Table Expression
            in SQL Server.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT IS SIMPLE CTE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is a Simple CTE?
            </h2>

            <p>
                A simple CTE is a Common Table Expression that defines a
                temporary result set and uses that result in the query
                immediately following the CTE.
            </p>

            <p class="mb-0">
                A simple CTE normally contains a single SELECT statement
                inside the CTE definition.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Basic Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH CTE_Name AS
(
    SELECT column1,
           column2
    FROM TableName
)
SELECT *
FROM CTE_Name;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         STEP BY STEP
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Understanding the Syntax
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Part</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <strong>WITH</strong>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Starts the CTE definition.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <strong>CTE_Name</strong>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Name assigned to the CTE.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <strong>AS</strong>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Introduces the query that defines the CTE.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <strong>SELECT</strong>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Retrieves the required columns.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <strong>FROM</strong>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Specifies the source table.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         TABLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example Table
            </h2>

            <p>
                Let's consider an <strong>Employees</strong> table:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>EmployeeId | EmployeeName | Department | Salary
----------------------------------------------------
101        | Amit          | IT         | 60000
102        | Rahul         | HR         | 45000
103        | Priya         | IT         | 70000
104        | Sneha         | Finance    | 55000</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SIMPLE CTE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Simple CTE Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we want to retrieve all employees from the
                <strong>IT</strong> department.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH ITEmployees AS
(
    SELECT EmployeeId,
           EmployeeName,
           Department,
           Salary
    FROM Employees
    WHERE Department = 'IT'
)
SELECT *
FROM ITEmployees;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         RESULT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Result
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">

                        <tr>
                            <th>EmployeeId</th>
                            <th>EmployeeName</th>
                            <th>Department</th>
                            <th>Salary</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>101</td>
                            <td>Amit</td>
                            <td>IT</td>
                            <td>60000</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>103</td>
                            <td>Priya</td>
                            <td>IT</td>
                            <td>70000</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CTE WITH WHERE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                CTE with WHERE Clause
            </h2>

            <p>
                A WHERE condition can be used inside the CTE to filter
                the data before the main query executes.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH HighSalaryEmployees AS
(
    SELECT EmployeeId,
           EmployeeName,
           Salary
    FROM Employees
    WHERE Salary &gt; 50000
)
SELECT *
FROM HighSalaryEmployees;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CTE WITH SELECTED COLUMNS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Selecting Specific Columns
            </h2>

            <p>
                We do not always need to select every column. We can
                select only the columns required by the application.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH EmployeeDetails AS
(
    SELECT EmployeeName,
           Department
    FROM Employees
)
SELECT *
FROM EmployeeDetails;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CTE WITH ORDER BY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Using CTE Result with ORDER BY
            </h2>

            <p>
                The main query can sort the data returned by the CTE.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH EmployeeDetails AS
(
    SELECT EmployeeId,
           EmployeeName,
           Salary
    FROM Employees
)
SELECT *
FROM EmployeeDetails
ORDER BY Salary DESC;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CTE WITH AGGREGATE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Simple CTE with Aggregate Function
            </h2>

            <p>
                A CTE can also contain aggregate functions such as
                COUNT, SUM and AVG.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>WITH DepartmentSalary AS
(
    SELECT Department,
           AVG(Salary) AS AverageSalary
    FROM Employees
    GROUP BY Department
)
SELECT *
FROM DepartmentSalary;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ADVANTAGES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Advantages of Simple CTE
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Makes complex queries easier to read.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Provides a meaningful name to an intermediate result.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Helps organize query logic.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Makes query maintenance easier.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Can be combined with filtering, joins and aggregation.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning shadow-sm mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li class="mb-2">
                A CTE starts with the <strong>WITH</strong> keyword.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                The CTE definition is enclosed inside parentheses.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                The query immediately following the CTE uses its result.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                A CTE is not a permanent database object.
            </li>

            <li>
                A simple CTE is useful for improving query readability.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-5">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p>
                A simple CTE is a temporary named result set created using
                the <strong>WITH</strong> keyword. It allows us to separate
                query logic and makes SQL statements easier to understand.
            </p>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In the next topic, we will learn how to use a
                <strong>CTE with JOIN</strong>.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


